            <ArticleTitle>How effective is the computer-based clinical practice guideline?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>24-33</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The primary prevention of coronary artery disease in patients with diabetes could have a large impact on health care costs and outcomes. Guidelines for improving diabetic health indices are common, but significant challenges exist in implementing them.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="GENERAL QUESTION" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">How does integrating an evidence-based guideline into an electronic medical record affect patient care?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SPECIFIC RESEARCH CHALLENGE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">How can we implement the new guideline-enhanced medical record in a controlled manner and measure its impact on physician satisfaction, diabetes process measures, and the risk for cardiovascular disease?</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PROPOSED APPROACH" NlmCategory="UNASSIGNED">All patients in the University of Washington system have an electronic Web-based medical record. Patients with diabetes will be randomly assigned to a guideline-enhanced or standard electronic medical record. The electronic medical record allows measurement of most clinical process measures and outcomes. Physician satisfaction will be measured by survey.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POTENTIAL DIFFICULTIES" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Contamination may occur when guideline recommendations are applied to control patients as physicians gain experience with the guideline-enhanced record.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
